    {
      "id": "ituYJj5hdFK0VHwybOCfi",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 277.9807552568095,
      "y": 1012.8131597397203,
      "width": 942.7627873861902,
      "height": 706.0385193983648,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#eebefa",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b07",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 82440605,
      "version": 3718,
      "versionNonce": 728150836,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782725168538,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) Cron vs Interval\n    - Cron expressions for calendar‑style schedules; ISO 8601 intervals like PT3H for “every 3\nhours”.\n- Scheduler converts these to exact timestamps and stores them as minute‑level epoch in\nTask_Schedule.\n\n2) Write‑heavy DB choice\n- Jobs, schedules, and run history generate heavy writes and relatively simple reads.\n- Cassandra/DynamoDB/Bigtable are good fits; we use denormalized tables without complex\ntransactions.\n\n3) Time‑bucket + segment partitioning\n- Task_Schedule partitions by (next_execution_time, segment) so “tasks for this minute and this\nsegment” are in one partition, enabling efficient minute‑level polling without scanning whole cluster.\n\n4) Segment‑based load distribution\n- Master assigns segments to scheduling workers; workers query only their segments, eliminating\nrace conditions and roughly balancing tasks / workers.\n\n5) Queue vs Kafka\n- Kafka: good for high‑throughput, partitioned consumption; ordering guaranteed within partition.\n- If global ordering is critical or you want simpler semantics, an alternative is FIFO SQS or\nRabbitMQ; trade‑off is throughput vs ordering guarantees.\n\n6) At‑least‑once & idempotency\n- Use job_id + scheduled_time or a dedicated run_id as idempotency key for JobRun.\n- If the same run message is processed twice, execution logic checks existing JobRun state to\navoid double business side‑effects.\n",
      "fontSize": 19.47692467305834,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) Cron vs Interval\n    - Cron expressions for calendar‑style schedules; ISO 8601 intervals like PT3H for “every 3 hours”.\n- Scheduler converts these to exact timestamps and stores them as minute‑level epoch in Task_Schedule.\n\n2) Write‑heavy DB choice\n- Jobs, schedules, and run history generate heavy writes and relatively simple reads.\n- Cassandra/DynamoDB/Bigtable are good fits; we use denormalized tables without complex transactions.\n\n3) Time‑bucket + segment partitioning\n- Task_Schedule partitions by (next_execution_time, segment) so “tasks for this minute and this segment” are in one partition, enabling efficient minute‑level polling without scanning whole cluster.\n\n4) Segment‑based load distribution\n- Master assigns segments to scheduling workers; workers query only their segments, eliminating race conditions and roughly balancing tasks / workers.\n\n5) Queue vs Kafka\n- Kafka: good for high‑throughput, partitioned consumption; ordering guaranteed within partition.\n- If global ordering is critical or you want simpler semantics, an alternative is FIFO SQS or RabbitMQ; trade‑off is throughput vs ordering guarantees.\n\n6) At‑least‑once & idempotency\n- Use job_id + scheduled_time or a dedicated run_id as idempotency key for JobRun.\n- If the same run message is processed twice, execution logic checks existing JobRun state to avoid double business side‑effects.\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "HSqc5H8eZJNjarL9VIP-z",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 291.9145434101533,
      "y": -370.67651071843227,
      "width": 916.2693684895834,
      "height": 1350,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0I",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1106172592,
      "version": 11620,
      "versionNonce": 797877172,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782658771988,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "\n1) Functional Requirements:  \n- Schedule job based on certain schedule and see the status of the job\n- Manual trigger of a job\n- For now every job will be a python script\n- can be recurring also, there should be supportfor disabling the job runs.\n- Support for dependency job\n\n----- out of scope -----\n- support of resouce allocation config\n- monitoring dashboard support\n- log monitoring support\n\n2) Scale Understanding \n- ~10B jobs per day\n\n2) Non Functional Requirements (SPARCS):  \n- Highly schalable ~10B jobs per day\n- job should run within 4s of scheduled time. If there is a further delay the user should be\nalerted.\n- Availability: high availability in terms of the creating and managing schedules\n- System should ensure atleast once execution of job\n- even if the system is down the jobs recieved should not be lost.\n- Eventual Consistency in terms of the changes to chedules etc\n\n3) BOE Calculations \n- Throughput Expectations\n    > 10B jobs/day => 10k jobs/sec\n\n- Sotrage expectation\n    > char(~2bytes) => 100lines scprit => each line contains ~100chars =>  20kb/ per script\n    > 1B(assumed unique job) * 20kb => ~20Tb of storage.\n\n4) Core Entities\n- User\n- Task: py script with logic to execute \n- Job: Instance of Scheduled task\n-  JobSchedule: next execution entry for jobs\n- JobRun: execution history \n\n5) API Design \n\nPOST/job/create\n    Input: {task_id, Cron, Parameters}\n    Output:{2xx, success}\n\nGET/job/status/:id\n    Output:{jobId, status: waiting|running|Success|fail} \n\nPOST /api/v1/users/{user_id}/jobs/{job_id}/trigger -- manual trigger \n\nPATCH /api/v1/users/{user_id}/jobs/{job_id}\n    Input: { \"enabled\": false }\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\n1) Functional Requirements:  \n- Schedule job based on certain schedule and see the status of the job\n- Manual trigger of a job\n- For now every job will be a python script\n- can be recurring also, there should be supportfor disabling the job runs.\n- Support for dependency job\n\n----- out of scope -----\n- support of resouce allocation config\n- monitoring dashboard support\n- log monitoring support\n\n2) Scale Understanding \n- ~10B jobs per day\n\n2) Non Functional Requirements (SPARCS):  \n- Highly schalable ~10B jobs per day\n- job should run within 4s of scheduled time. If there is a further delay the user should be alerted.\n- Availability: high availability in terms of the creating and managing schedules\n- System should ensure atleast once execution of job\n- even if the system is down the jobs recieved should not be lost.\n- Eventual Consistency in terms of the changes to chedules etc\n\n3) BOE Calculations \n- Throughput Expectations\n    > 10B jobs/day => 10k jobs/sec\n\n- Sotrage expectation\n    > char(~2bytes) => 100lines scprit => each line contains ~100chars =>  20kb/ per script\n    > 1B(assumed unique job) * 20kb => ~20Tb of storage.\n\n4) Core Entities\n- User\n- Task: py script with logic to execute \n- Job: Instance of Scheduled task\n-  JobSchedule: next execution entry for jobs\n- JobRun: execution history \n\n5) API Design \n\nPOST/job/create\n    Input: {task_id, Cron, Parameters}\n    Output:{2xx, success}\n\nGET/job/status/:id\n    Output:{jobId, status: waiting|running|Success|fail} \n\nPOST /api/v1/users/{user_id}/jobs/{job_id}/trigger -- manual trigger \n\nPATCH /api/v1/users/{user_id}/jobs/{job_id}\n    Input: { \"enabled\": false }\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
