    {
      "id": "aoui3fXAGxZFf4vIU88Wf",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1339.2577903831266,
      "y": 3374.0598257876686,
      "width": 919.4992215988109,
      "height": 400,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#eebefa",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bQ4",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1744401587,
      "version": 1198,
      "versionNonce": 25572788,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782586032470,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) What Are RSS Feeds?\n- Imagine every news website had a standardized notice board where they automatically post\na short summary every time they publish a new story. Instead of you visiting 10,000\nwebsites to check for news, each site keeps its own tidy list that any program can read.\n- That \"standardized notice board\" is called an RSS feed (RSS stands for Really Simple\nSyndication).\n- An RSS feed is just a special file (written in a format called XML) that lists recent articles.\nAn RSS feed is designed for computers to read — it's plain, structured, and predictable.\n\n2) Crawling and Polling\n- Polling = regularly re-checking each RSS feed (e.g., \"every 5 minutes, look for new items\").\nSimple and effective.\n- Crawling = a program that visits webpages and reads their content directly. Used when a\npublisher doesn't offer an RSS feed.\n- Be respectful: good crawlers obey robots.txt (a file where sites say what's allowed) and\ndon't hammer servers too fast. Learn more from Google's crawling documentation.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) What Are RSS Feeds?\n- Imagine every news website had a standardized notice board where they automatically post a short summary every time they publish a new story. Instead of you visiting 10,000 websites to check for news, each site keeps its own tidy list that any program can read.\n- That \"standardized notice board\" is called an RSS feed (RSS stands for Really Simple Syndication).\n- An RSS feed is just a special file (written in a format called XML) that lists recent articles. An RSS feed is designed for computers to read — it's plain, structured, and predictable.\n\n2) Crawling and Polling\n- Polling = regularly re-checking each RSS feed (e.g., \"every 5 minutes, look for new items\"). Simple and effective.\n- Crawling = a program that visits webpages and reads their content directly. Used when a publisher doesn't offer an RSS feed.\n- Be respectful: good crawlers obey robots.txt (a file where sites say what's allowed) and don't hammer servers too fast. Learn more from Google's crawling documentation.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "2Or6gXU876BwqjoLWgqYw",
