      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bQ5",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 249337693,
      "version": 12075,
      "versionNonce": 87485364,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782723633774,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) Schedule a job based on a certain schedule and see the status of the job\n    - Scheduling: When a user creates a job, the Scheduler Plane (job_service) saves the job\ndetails and its next_execution_time in a database (like Cassandra). A master-worker setup\n(scheduling_service) checks this schedule every minute. When a job's time arrives, the workers\npush the task into a message queue (like a Kafka topic).\n    - Seeing Status: The Execution Plane is responsible for status updates. As the\nexecution_service processes the task, it updates the Task_Execution_History database\ntable, changing the job's status from SCHEDULED to RUNNING, and finally to COMPLETED\nor FAILED.\n\n2) Manual trigger of a job\n    - Manual triggers reuse the exact same flow but skip the waiting period. When a user\nmanually triggers a job via the job_service, it bypasses the minute-by-minute schedule check\nand pushes the task directly into the same Kafka topic. The execution_service then picks it\nup and runs it immediately.\n\n3. For now, every job will be a Python script\n    - When the execution_service consumes a message from the queue, it fetches the specific\nPython script associated with that task from blob storage. It then spins up an isolated\nDocker container (using a container runtime like ECS or Kubernetes) to safely execute the\nPython script with the necessary parameters.\n\n4. Can be recurring also; there should be support for disabling job runs\n    - Recurring: For jobs that run repeatedly, the system continuously updates the\nTask_Schedule database table with the calculated next_execution_time once the current run\nis scheduled.\n    - Disabling: To pause or disable a job, the job_service updates the job's record in the\ndatabase. The scheduling_service will then ignore this job during its minute-by-minute checks\nuntil it is re-enabled.\n\n5. Support for dependency jobs\n    - This is handled through dependency logic shared between the scheduler and execution\nplanes. When a primary job completes successfully, the execution_service updates the status.\nThe scheduling_service detects this completion, verifies if any dependent jobs are waiting on\nit, and if so, schedules or pushes those dependent tasks into the queue to be executed\nnext.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) Schedule a job based on a certain schedule and see the status of the job\n    - Scheduling: When a user creates a job, the Scheduler Plane (job_service) saves the job details and its next_execution_time in a database (like Cassandra). A master-worker setup (scheduling_service) checks this schedule every minute. When a job's time arrives, the workers push the task into a message queue (like a Kafka topic).\n    - Seeing Status: The Execution Plane is responsible for status updates. As the execution_service processes the task, it updates the Task_Execution_History database table, changing the job's status from SCHEDULED to RUNNING, and finally to COMPLETED or FAILED.\n\n2) Manual trigger of a job\n    - Manual triggers reuse the exact same flow but skip the waiting period. When a user manually triggers a job via the job_service, it bypasses the minute-by-minute schedule check and pushes the task directly into the same Kafka topic. The execution_service then picks it up and runs it immediately.\n\n3. For now, every job will be a Python script\n    - When the execution_service consumes a message from the queue, it fetches the specific Python script associated with that task from blob storage. It then spins up an isolated Docker container (using a container runtime like ECS or Kubernetes) to safely execute the Python script with the necessary parameters.\n\n4. Can be recurring also; there should be support for disabling job runs\n    - Recurring: For jobs that run repeatedly, the system continuously updates the Task_Schedule database table with the calculated next_execution_time once the current run is scheduled.\n    - Disabling: To pause or disable a job, the job_service updates the job's record in the database. The scheduling_service will then ignore this job during its minute-by-minute checks until it is re-enabled.\n\n5. Support for dependency jobs\n    - This is handled through dependency logic shared between the scheduler and execution planes. When a primary job completes successfully, the execution_service updates the status. The scheduling_service detects this completion, verifies if any dependent jobs are waiting on it, and if so, schedules or pushes those dependent tasks into the queue to be executed next.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "DQyIsz7cSPhloijNQ88Rx",
