    },
    {
      "id": "v60r_sXFsJS07uC1Rxp0r",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1292.6606318070303,
      "y": -348.3750070851206,
      "width": 916.2693684895834,
      "height": 800,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bUj",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 269983933,
      "version": 10598,
      "versionNonce": 721148684,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782571550058,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "\n> Question: What kind of payment system are we building?\n- Answer: A payment backend for an e-commerce application (similar to Amazon) that handles\nall money movement when a customer places an order.\n\n> Question: What payment options should be supported?\n- Answer: While it supports multiple options like PayPal and bank cards in real life, the primary\nfocus for this scenario will be credit card payments.\n\n> Question: Will we handle credit card payment processing ourselves?\n- Answer: No, the system will integrate with third-party payment processors such as Stripe,\nBraintree, or Square.\n\n> Question: Will the system store sensitive credit card data?\n- Answer: No, for security and compliance reasons, credit card numbers are not stored\ninternally. The third-party processors will handle the sensitive data.\n\n> Question: Is the application global, and does it support multiple currencies?\n- Answer: Yes, the application is global, but for the scope of this design, we will assume it\noperates using only a single currency.\n\n> Question: What is the expected transaction volume?\n- Answer: The system is expected to handle 1 million transactions per day.\n\n> Question: Does the system need to support a pay-out flow to pay sellers?\n- Answer: Yes, the system must support a pay-out flow to disburse funds to sellers every\nmonth.\n\n> Question: Are there any additional operational or system requirements to consider?\n- Answer: Yes, because the system interacts with various internal (accounting, analytics) and\nexternal services, it must include a reconciliation process to identify and resolve any data\ninconsistencies caused by service failures.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\n> Question: What kind of payment system are we building?\n- Answer: A payment backend for an e-commerce application (similar to Amazon) that handles all money movement when a customer places an order.\n\n> Question: What payment options should be supported?\n- Answer: While it supports multiple options like PayPal and bank cards in real life, the primary \nfocus for this scenario will be credit card payments.\n\n> Question: Will we handle credit card payment processing ourselves?\n- Answer: No, the system will integrate with third-party payment processors such as Stripe, Braintree, or Square.\n\n> Question: Will the system store sensitive credit card data?\n- Answer: No, for security and compliance reasons, credit card numbers are not stored internally. The third-party processors will handle the sensitive data.\n\n> Question: Is the application global, and does it support multiple currencies?\n- Answer: Yes, the application is global, but for the scope of this design, we will assume it operates using only a single currency.\n\n> Question: What is the expected transaction volume?\n- Answer: The system is expected to handle 1 million transactions per day.\n\n> Question: Does the system need to support a pay-out flow to pay sellers?\n- Answer: Yes, the system must support a pay-out flow to disburse funds to sellers every month.\n\n> Question: Are there any additional operational or system requirements to consider?\n- Answer: Yes, because the system interacts with various internal (accounting, analytics) and external services, it must include a reconciliation process to identify and resolve any data inconsistencies caused by service failures.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
