    {
      "id": "mOj3dLaaSwnIcf3Ns2xG8",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1243.2879813648576,
      "y": 611.7402154596139,
      "width": 942.7627873861902,
      "height": 1460.7693504793754,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#eebefa",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bbP",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1643183628,
      "version": 3826,
      "versionNonce": 1276887860,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782725161633,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) DB Schema & Partitioning\n- Jobs table:\n    > Partition key: user_id\n    > Clustering key: job_id\n    > Stores job config including executionInterval, maxRetryCount, is_recurring.\n- Task_Schedule:\n    > Initially: user_id partition, job_id sort, next_execution_time field. But that leads to\ncluster‑wide scans.\n    > Optimized: convert next_execution_time to minute‑level epoch; use composite partition key\n(next_execution_time, segment), sort by job_id.\n    > This ensures tasks for the current minute and segment live together; each worker scans only\nits partitions.\n- Task_Execution_History:\n    > Partition key job_id; cluster key execution_time.\n    > Contains status, retry_count, last_update_time, etc.\n    > You can explicitly walk through why the first Task_Schedule design is inefficient and how the\noptimized partitioning fixes it.\n\n2) Scaling Scheduling & Execution\n- Scheduling:\n    > Single scheduler instance cannot handle ~35k–100k tasks/min + becomes single point of\nfailure.\n    > Solution: master + multiple workers, with segments:\n    > Master assigns segments; workers query Task_Schedule with segment in (worker_segments)\nfor each minute.\n    > Master monitors workers via heartbeat and reassigns segments on failure.\n\n- Execution:\n    > execution_service scaled horizontally as Kafka consumer group on task topic.\n    > More partitions + more instances ⇒ more parallelism.\n    > Each execution instance runs scripts in containers, updates history, and drives retry pipeline.\n\n3) Retry Mechanism & DLQ\n- Retry logic:\n    > Check job’s max_retry_count and is_retryable.\n    > If still retryable, increment retry_count in Task_Execution_History and send message to\ntask_retry topic.\n    > Reads from task_retry, applies delay (fixed or incremental/backoff), re‑executes task. On\nsuccess, mark COMPLETED.\n    > On failure again, if still below limit, may send to another retry topic (task_retry_2) to avoid\nblocking other tasks.\n    > Once retry_count exceeds max_retry_count, mark FAILED and send to task_dlq. DLQ is for\nmanual inspection, alerting, and potential manual reprocessing.\n    > You can highlight: this gives a robust way to implement at‑least‑once semantics with\ncontrolled retries and bounded failure, instead of infinite retry loops.\n\n4) Dependencies Between Jobs\n- Dependency checking in scheduler:\n    > Job config contains dependencies: [jobA, jobB].\n    > Before scheduler enqueues a run for jobC at time T, it checks Task_Execution_History for\njobA and jobB to ensure they have COMPLETED status for the relevant window.\n\n- Event‑driven dependencies:\n    > When jobA completes, scheduler emits a Task_Schedule entry for jobB/C.\n    > This pushes dependency logic into completion path rather than checking at scheduling time.\n\n- Either way, emphasize:\n    > Dependency graph must be acyclic (validate at job creation).\n    > Failures in upstream jobs should either block downstream jobs or trigger failure for them as\nwell, depending on business requirements.",
      "fontSize": 19.47692467305834,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) DB Schema & Partitioning\n- Jobs table:\n    > Partition key: user_id\n    > Clustering key: job_id\n    > Stores job config including executionInterval, maxRetryCount, is_recurring.\n- Task_Schedule:\n    > Initially: user_id partition, job_id sort, next_execution_time field. But that leads to cluster‑wide scans.\n    > Optimized: convert next_execution_time to minute‑level epoch; use composite partition key (next_execution_time, segment), sort by job_id.\n    > This ensures tasks for the current minute and segment live together; each worker scans only its partitions.\n- Task_Execution_History:\n    > Partition key job_id; cluster key execution_time.\n    > Contains status, retry_count, last_update_time, etc.\n    > You can explicitly walk through why the first Task_Schedule design is inefficient and how the optimized partitioning fixes it.\n\n2) Scaling Scheduling & Execution\n- Scheduling:\n    > Single scheduler instance cannot handle ~35k–100k tasks/min + becomes single point of failure.\n    > Solution: master + multiple workers, with segments:\n    > Master assigns segments; workers query Task_Schedule with segment in (worker_segments) for each minute.\n    > Master monitors workers via heartbeat and reassigns segments on failure.\n\n- Execution:\n    > execution_service scaled horizontally as Kafka consumer group on task topic.\n    > More partitions + more instances ⇒ more parallelism.\n    > Each execution instance runs scripts in containers, updates history, and drives retry pipeline.\n\n3) Retry Mechanism & DLQ\n- Retry logic:\n    > Check job’s max_retry_count and is_retryable.\n    > If still retryable, increment retry_count in Task_Execution_History and send message to task_retry topic.\n    > Reads from task_retry, applies delay (fixed or incremental/backoff), re‑executes task. On success, mark COMPLETED.\n    > On failure again, if still below limit, may send to another retry topic (task_retry_2) to avoid blocking other tasks.\n    > Once retry_count exceeds max_retry_count, mark FAILED and send to task_dlq. DLQ is for manual inspection, alerting, and potential manual reprocessing.\n    > You can highlight: this gives a robust way to implement at‑least‑once semantics with controlled retries and bounded failure, instead of infinite retry loops.\n\n4) Dependencies Between Jobs\n- Dependency checking in scheduler:\n    > Job config contains dependencies: [jobA, jobB].\n    > Before scheduler enqueues a run for jobC at time T, it checks Task_Execution_History for jobA and jobB to ensure they have COMPLETED status for the relevant window.\n\n- Event‑driven dependencies:\n    > When jobA completes, scheduler emits a Task_Schedule entry for jobB/C.\n    > This pushes dependency logic into completion path rather than checking at scheduling time.\n\n- Either way, emphasize:\n    > Dependency graph must be acyclic (validate at job creation).\n    > Failures in upstream jobs should either block downstream jobs or trigger failure for them as well, depending on business requirements.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
